 "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Bill Lee (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Tennessee's open governor's race pits Republican Senator Marsha Blackburn against Democratic nominee Jerri Green, a comparatively little-known candidate, in a state with a 14-point Republican lean. Bill Lee is term-limited, and the office has stayed in Republican hands. Six raters unanimously place the race in the Safe or Solid Republican column, and prediction markets give Blackburn roughly a 95% chance of winning.\n\nPolling has been sparse but consistent: the two most recent surveys show Blackburn ahead by nine and thirteen points among registered and likely voters. A crowded field of independent candidates, including Lauren Pinkston, has drawn attention and prompted the Democratic nominee to ask one independent to withdraw, but nothing suggests a genuine contest. No debates are scheduled, and the campaign has generated limited statewide engagement, consistent with expectations of a comfortable Republican hold.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0257, "margin": -11.12}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0463, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0186, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0193, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0323, "margin": -11.0}]}